WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
Injection happens when untrusted input is interpreted as instructions or structure in the

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.755
wrong context.

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:09.200
JavaScript applications face this risk in many places because they interact with

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.225
databases,

00:00:10.225 --> 00:00:15.545
document stores, shells, template engines, browsers, parsers, URLs, headers, file paths,

00:00:15.545 --> 00:00:16.800
and downstream APIs.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.990
The variety of contexts is what makes injection prevention a design discipline rather than

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:21.775
a single trick.

00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:24.080
The most important habit is data flow awareness.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.925
Know where untrusted data enters, how it is transformed, where it is stored,

00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:29.500
and which sensitive sinks it reaches.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.500
Client-side validation can improve usability, but server-side validation,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.000
safe construction, and context-aware output handling are still required.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.385
A value that is safe in one context may be unsafe in another.

00:00:40.385 --> 00:00:42.495
Strong prevention combines several controls.

00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:44.260
Use parameterized queries for SQL.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.685
Validate object shape and allowlisted fields for document queries.

00:00:47.685 --> 00:00:49.460
Avoid string-built shell commands.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.800
Keep user content separate from templates and executable logic.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.500
Use safe DOM APIs and context-aware encoding for browser rendering.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.500
Apply least privilege so one mistake does not become a system-wide failure.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:02.676
Testing and review keep these controls alive.

00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:06.525
Security-focused unit and integration tests, schema tests, negative tests,

00:01:06.525 --> 00:01:11.350
dependency review, static analysis, and code review all help identify risky paths.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.390
Documentation helps future developers understand why a particular sink needs a particular

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.000
control.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.700
The goal is not memorizing payloads.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.620
The goal is designing JavaScript applications where untrusted data cannot change command

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.140
meaning.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:26.400
When developers keep data as data, choose safe APIs, validate intent,

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.620
and encode for the correct context, injection risk drops dramatically.
